// Official JKBOSE / CBSE / NCERT Syllabus Alignment
// Gold Standard Reference Textbook & 3-Tab Architecture (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard)

export const c11Phy6HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- CHAPTER TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Unit VI: Gravitation (06 Marks)\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Reference Textbook Guide &bull; JKBOSE / CBSE Official Syllabus Alignment\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      ⚡ Quick Glossary & Fundamental Gravitational Quantities\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Universal Gravitational Constant (G):</b> Universal proportionality constant; G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>. Dimensions: <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Acceleration Due to Gravity (g):</b> Gravitational acceleration near Earth&apos;s surface; g = GM/R<sup>2</sup> &approx; 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>. Dimensions: <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Gravitational Potential (V):</b> Work done per unit mass in bringing a test body from infinity to a point; V = -GM/r. SI Unit: <b>J/kg</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Escape Speed (v<sub>e</sub>):</b> Minimum launch speed required to permanently escape gravitational pull; v_e = &radic;(2gR) &approx; 11.2 km/s on Earth.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    1. Kepler&apos;s Laws of Planetary Motion\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    For centuries, celestial mechanics struggled between geocentric dogma and early heliocentric models. Utilizing decades of meticulous astronomical observations recorded by Tycho Brahe, Johannes Kepler deduced three empirical laws that revolutionized our comprehension of planetary mechanics, subsequently laying the experimental foundation for Isaac Newton&apos;s universal synthesis.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(i) Kepler&apos;s First Law (Law of Orbits):</b><br/>\n    <i>Every planet revolves around the Sun in an elliptical orbit, with the Sun situated at one of the two foci of the ellipse.</i>\n    <br/>An ellipse is a geometric conic section characterized by a semi-major axis a, semi-minor axis b, and eccentricity e = &radic;(1 - b<sup>2</sup>/a<sup>2</sup>) (0 &lt; e &lt; 1). The closest approach of the planet to the Sun is termed the <b>perihelion</b> (r_{\\min} = a(1 - e)), while the farthest point is termed the <b>aphelion</b> (r_{\\max} = a(1 + e)). Planetary orbits in our Solar System possess low eccentricities (Earth&apos;s e &approx; 0.0167), approximating near-circular geometries.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(ii) Kepler&apos;s Second Law (Law of Areas):</b><br/>\n    <i>The radius vector drawn from the Sun to any planet sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time; that is, the areal velocity of the planet is strictly constant.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FDC830; color: #1E293B; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Areal Velocity</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The rate at which area is swept out by the position vector of a planet relative to the Sun: dA/dt = L / (2m) = constant, representing an exact consequence of the conservation of angular momentum under central forces.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of Kepler's Second Law from Angular Momentum Conservation</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Central Nature of Gravitational Force</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The gravitational pull of the Sun on a planet acts directly along the radial direction connecting them: <b>F</b> = -(G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}. Because <b>r</b> and <b>F</b> are strictly collinear, the torque about the Sun vanishes identically: $<b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>0</b></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Angular Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since <b>&tau;</b> = (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b>, the planet&apos;s orbital angular momentum <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b> = m(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>) is a strict constant of motion.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Differential Area Swept Out</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In infinitesimal time dt, the radius vector sweeps out a triangular area: d<b>A</b> = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; d<b>r</b>) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>\\,dt)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Areal Velocity Expression</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Dividing by dt: (d<b>A</b> / dt) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>) = (<b>L</b> / 2m) = constant As a physical consequence, a planet moves fastest when closest to the Sun at perihelion (r \\downarrow &rArr; v \\uparrow) and slowest at aphelion (r \\uparrow &rArr; v \\downarrow).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(iii) Kepler&apos;s Third Law (Law of Periods):</b><br/>\n    <i>The square of the orbital period of revolution of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of its elliptical orbit:</i>\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFE082; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    T<sup>2</sup> &prop; a<sup>3</sup> &nbsp;&implies;&nbsp; (T<sup>2</sup> / a<sup>3</sup>) = constant\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    For circular orbits of radius r, centripetal balance yields (m v<sup>2</sup> / r) = (G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; v = &radic;{(G M_s / r)}. Period T = (2&pi; r / v) = {2&pi; r<sup>3/2</sup>}{&radic;(G M_s)}, whence squaring gives:\n    T<sup>2</sup> = ((4&pi;<sup>2</sup> / G M_s)) r<sup>3</sup>\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Universal Law of Gravitation\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In 1687, Sir Isaac Newton formulated the overarching principle governing all gravitational interactions in the universe. Gravitational attraction is not confined to terrestrial bodies but extends infinitely across cosmic space.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FDC830; color: #1E293B; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Every particle of matter in the universe attracts every other particle with a force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Newton (N)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Mathematical Formulation:</b><br/>\n    For two point masses m_1 and m_2 separated by distance r:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFE082; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    F = G (m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    In vector form, the gravitational force <b>F</b>_{12} exerted by mass m_1 on mass m_2 located at position vector <b>r</b>_{12} is:\n    <b>F</b>_{12} = -(G m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}_{12}\n    The negative sign indicates that gravity is exclusively an <b>attractive force</b>. In accordance with Newton&apos;s third law, <b>F</b>_{21} = -<b>F</b>_{12}.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.06); border-left: 4px solid #0EA5E9; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #0EA5E9; font-size: 18px;\">💡</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px;\">Intuitive Analogy &bull; Universal Gravitational Constant vs Acceleration Due to Gravity</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Think of G as the universal cosmic currency exchange rate—it is identical everywhere in the cosmos (on Earth, Jupiter, or deep interstellar space). In contrast, g is local purchasing power, varying drastically whether you stand at Earth's sea level, atop Mount Everest, or on the surface of the Moon.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Acceleration Due to Gravity (g) & Mean Density of Earth\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    When a body of mass m is released freely near Earth&apos;s surface, it accelerates downwards under Earth&apos;s gravitational pull. Equating Newton&apos;s gravitational attraction to the body&apos;s weight:\n    F = m g = (G M m / R<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>)\n    where M = 5.972 &times; 10<sup>24</sup> kg is the mass of Earth, and R = 6.371 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m is Earth&apos;s mean equatorial radius.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Mean Density of Earth (&rho;):</b><br/>\n    Assuming Earth to be a uniform sphere of radius R and density &rho;, its mass is M = (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;. Substituting this into the formula for g:\n    g = (G / R<sup>2</sup>)((4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;) = (4 / 3)&pi; G R &rho;\n    Rearranging gives the mean density of Earth:\n    &rho; = (3 g / 4&pi; G R) &approx; 5.5 &times; 10<sup>3</sup> kg/m<sup>3</sup>\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. Variation of Acceleration Due to Gravity with Altitude and Depth\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    The value of acceleration due to gravity g is not universally constant across terrestrial locations; it varies systematically with height above sea level, depth beneath the crust, latitude, and Earth&apos;s non-spherical oblate spheroidal shape.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(i) Variation with Altitude (Height h above Earth's Surface):</b>\n  </p>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of g at Altitude h</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Exact Expression at Distance r = R + h</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At height h above Earth&apos;s surface, the distance from Earth&apos;s center is r = R + h. The local acceleration due to gravity is: g_h = (G M / (R + h)<sup>2</sup>) = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>(1 + (h / R))<sup>2</sup>) = (g / (1 + (h / R))<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Binomial Approximation for Small Altitudes (h << R)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Rewriting in negative power form: g_h = g(1 + (h / R))<sup>-2</sup>. Expanding binomially when h \\ll R and neglecting higher-order terms: g_h &approx; g(1 - (2h / R))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Fractional and Percentage Decrease</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Fractional decrease: (g - g_h / g) = (2h / R).<br/>Percentage decrease: ((&Delta;g / g)) &times; 100 = ((2h / R)) &times; 100\\%.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(ii) Variation with Depth (d beneath Earth's Surface):</b>\n  </p>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of g at Depth d</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Newtonian Spherical Shell Theorem</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At depth d below the surface, the distance from the center is r = R - d. According to Gauss&apos;s Law for gravity and Newton&apos;s Shell Theorem, the outer concentric spherical shell of thickness d exerts <b>zero net gravitational force</b> on any interior point. The gravitational pull is produced solely by the inner core of radius r = R - d.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Inner Sphere Mass Expression</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Assuming uniform density &rho;, the enclosed mass is: M&apos; = (4 / 3)&pi; (R - d)<sup>3</sup> &rho;</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Evaluating g_d</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The acceleration due to gravity is: g_d = (G M&apos; / (R - d)<sup>2</sup>) = (G / (R - d)<sup>2</sup>)[(4 / 3)&pi; (R - d)<sup>3</sup> &rho;] = (4 / 3)&pi; G &rho; (R - d) Since g = (4 / 3)&pi; G R &rho;, taking the ratio yields: g_d = g(1 - (d / R))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">At Earth's Center (d = R)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Setting d = R: g_{center} = g(1 - (R / R)) = 0. A body at the center of the Earth experiences complete gravitational weightlessness.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.07); border-left: 4px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #EF4444; font-size: 18px;\">⚠️</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px;\">Board Exam Pitfall &bull; Altitude vs Depth Rate of Decrease</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">For small distances (h, d << R), gravity drops TWICE as fast with altitude as it does with depth: g_h = g(1 - 2h/R) versus g_d = g(1 - d/R). Therefore, the decrease in g at a height h is equal to the decrease in g at a depth d = 2h.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Gravitational Potential Energy & Gravitational Potential\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Gravitational fields are conservative force fields. Consequently, the work performed against gravitational attraction is stored as gravitational potential energy, independent of the geometric trajectory traversed.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(i) Gravitational Potential Energy (U):</b><br/>\n    The <b>gravitational potential energy</b> of a body of mass m at distance r from Earth&apos;s center is defined as the work done by an external agent in bringing the mass from infinity to distance r without acceleration:\n    U(r) = -\\int_{\\infty}<sup>r</sup> <b>F</b> \\cdot d<b>r</b> = -\\int_{\\infty}<sup>r</sup> (G M m / x<sup>2</sup>)\\,dx = -G M m [-(1 / x)]_{\\infty}<sup>r</sup> = -(G M m / r)\n    The <b>negative sign</b> signifies that gravitational force is attractive; work must be supplied to liberate the body from Earth&apos;s gravitational grip to infinite separation (U = 0).\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(ii) Gravitational Potential (V):</b><br/>\n    Gravitational potential at a spatial point is defined as the potential energy per unit test mass:\n    V(r) = (U(r) / m) = -(G M / r)\n    The gravitational field intensity <b>E</b>_g and potential V are related via the spatial gradient:\n    <b>E</b>_g = -(dV / dr)\\hat{r}\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    6. Escape Speed\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    When a projectile is thrown vertically upwards, its kinetic energy is progressively converted into gravitational potential energy. At everyday speeds, it halts and plummets back. However, if projected with a sufficiently large critical speed, its kinetic energy overcomes the total negative potential energy, allowing it to escape Earth&apos;s gravitational influence entirely.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FDC830; color: #1E293B; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 16px;\">Escape Speed</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The minimum initial speed with which a body must be projected from the surface of a celestial body so that it just overcomes the gravitational field and never returns on its own.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">m/s (or km/s)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFE082; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of Escape Speed from Earth's Surface</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Initial Mechanical Energy at Surface (r = R)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let a mass m be launched with speed v_e from Earth&apos;s surface: E_i = K_i + U_i = (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Final Mechanical Energy at Infinity (r -> infinity)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At infinity, gravitational potential energy vanishes (U_f = 0). For minimum escape speed, the body just reaches infinity with zero residual velocity (K_f = 0): E_f = 0 + 0 = 0</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Total Mechanical Energy</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Equating initial and final mechanical energy: (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R) = 0 &rArr; (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> = (G M m / R) v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Expressing in terms of Surface Gravity g</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; G M = g R<sup>2</sup>: v_e = &radic;(2 g R)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Numerical Value for Earth & Planetary Significance</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For Earth (g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>, R = 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m): v_e = &radic;(2 &times; 9.8 &times; 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 11.2 km/s Notice that v_e is <b>independent of the mass of the projectile</b> and launch angle. On the Moon, v_e &approx; 2.38 km/s, which is lower than the root-mean-square thermal speeds of gas molecules (oxygen, nitrogen), explaining why the Moon has no atmosphere.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; border-bottom: 2px solid #FDC830; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Orbital Velocity of an Earth Satellite & Orbital Dynamics\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    An artificial satellite is an object placed into stable orbit around a celestial body. For circular orbital motion at altitude h (r = R + h), the gravitational pull provides the necessary centripetal force:\n    (m v_o<sup>2</sup> / r) = (G M m / r<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; v_o = &radic;{(G M / r)} = &radic;{(G M / R + h)}\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(i) Near-Earth Orbit Speed:</b><br/>\n    For satellites revolving very close to Earth&apos;s surface (h \\ll R):\n    v_o = &radic;{(G M / R)} = &radic;(g R) &approx; 7.92 km/s &approx; 8 km/s\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(ii) Relationship Between Escape Speed and Orbital Speed:</b><br/>\n    v_e = &radic;(2 g R) = &radic;(2) &times; &radic;(g R) = &radic;(2) v_o &approx; 1.414 v_o\n    An increase of only <b>41.4%</b> in the orbital speed of a near-Earth satellite causes it to break out of orbit and escape into interplanetary space.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(iii) Orbital Period (T):</b><br/>\n    T = (2&pi; r / v_o) = {2&pi; (R + h)<sup>3/2</sup>}{&radic;(G M)} = 2&pi; &radic;{((R + h)<sup>3</sup> / g R<sup>2</sup>)}\n    For a near-Earth satellite (h \\ll R): T &approx; 2&pi; &radic;{(R / g)} &approx; 84.6 minutes &approx; 1.4 hours.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">(iv) Energy Analysis of an Orbiting Satellite:</b><br/>\n    &bull; <b>Kinetic Energy:</b> K = (1 / 2)m v_o<sup>2</sup> = (G M m / 2 r)<br/>\n    &bull; <b>Potential Energy:</b> U = -(G M m / r)<br/>\n    &bull; <b>Total Mechanical Energy:</b> E = K + U = (G M m / 2 r) - (G M m / r) = -(G M m / 2 r)<br/>\n    &bull; <b>Binding Energy:</b> The energy required to liberate the satellite from its orbit: B.E. = -E = +(G M m / 2 r).\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- MASTER FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 2px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      📋 Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet &bull; Unit VI: Gravitation\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 13.5px; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Newton&apos;s Law of Gravitation:</b><br/>\n        F = G(m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>) &bull; G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup>/kg<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Surface Acceleration Due to Gravity:</b><br/>\n        g = (GM / R<sup>2</sup>) = (4 / 3)&pi; G R &rho; &bull; &rho; = (3g / 4&pi; GR)\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Gravity Variation with Altitude:</b><br/>\n        Exact: g_h = (g / (1 + h/R)<sup>2</sup>) &bull; (h \\ll R): g_h = g(1 - (2h / R))\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Gravity Variation with Depth:</b><br/>\n        g_d = g(1 - (d / R)) &bull; At center (d = R): g = 0\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Gravitational Potential & Potential Energy:</b><br/>\n        V = -(GM / r) &bull; U = -(GMm / r) &bull; E_g = -(dV / dr)\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Escape Speed & Orbital Velocity:</b><br/>\n        v_e = &radic;(2gR) &approx; 11.2 km/s &bull; v_o = &radic;(gR) &approx; 7.92 km/s<br/>\n        Relation: v_e = &radic;(2) v_o\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Kepler&apos;s Third Law & Satellite Period:</b><br/>\n        T<sup>2</sup> = ((4&pi;<sup>2</sup> / GM)) r<sup>3</sup> &bull; T = 2&pi; &radic;{((R+h)<sup>3</sup> / GM)}\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #FDC830;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Satellite Energy Balance:</b><br/>\n        K = (GMm / 2r), U = -(GMm / r), E = -(GMm / 2r), B.E. = +(GMm / 2r)$\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy6HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FDC830; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit VI: Gravitation (06 Marks) &bull; Official Syllabus Coverage\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION A: VERY SHORT ANSWER (VSA) QUESTIONS (1 MARK EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark Each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, universal constants, dimensional formulas, SI units, and instantaneous planetary facts.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q1 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q1: State Newton&apos;s Universal Law of Gravitation.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> Every particle of matter in the universe attracts every other particle with a mutual force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      F = G(m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q2 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q2: Write the vector form of Newton&apos;s law of gravitation and state the physical significance of the negative sign.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> In vector notation:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>F</b>_{12} = -(G m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}_{12}\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The <b>negative sign</b> indicates that the gravitational force is strictly <b>attractive</b> in nature, directed opposite to the unit position displacement vector \\hat{r}_{12}.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q3 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q3: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of the universal gravitational constant G.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> Newton metre-squared per kilogram-squared (<b>N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup></b> or <b>m<sup>3</sup> kg<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-2</sup></b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q4 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q4: Why is G called a &apos;universal constant&apos;, whereas g is not?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> <b>G</b> has the identical value everywhere in the cosmos, independent of time, temperature, chemical composition, and medium. In contrast, <b>g</b> depends on the mass and radius of the specific celestial body and varies with altitude, depth, and latitude.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q5 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q5: What is the accepted experimental value of universal gravitational constant G?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      $G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>\n      It was first experimentally measured in the laboratory by Henry Cavendish in 1798 using a sensitive torsion balance.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q6 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q6: State Kepler&apos;s First Law of Planetary Motion (Law of Orbits).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> Every planet revolves around the Sun in an <b>elliptical orbit</b>, with the Sun situated at one of the two foci of the ellipse.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q7 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q7: State Kepler&apos;s Second Law of Planetary Motion (Law of Areas).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The radius vector drawn from the Sun to any revolving planet sweeps out <b>equal areas in equal intervals of time</b>; that is, the areal velocity of the planet remains strictly constant (dA/dt = constant).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q8 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q8: Which universal conservation law forms the theoretical basis of Kepler&apos;s Second Law?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum</b>. Because the gravitational force exerted by the Sun is a strictly central force (<b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>0</b>), no external torque acts on the planet, so orbital angular momentum <b>L</b> is conserved.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q9 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q9: State Kepler&apos;s Third Law of Planetary Motion (Law of Periods).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The square of the orbital period of revolution T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis a of its elliptical orbit:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      T<sup>2</sup> &prop; a<sup>3</sup> &rArr; (T<sup>2</sup> / a<sup>3</sup>) = constant\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q10 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q10: Where does a planet move fastest in its orbit: at perihelion or aphelion? Justify.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> At <b>perihelion</b> (closest approach to the Sun). By conservation of angular momentum, L = m r v = constant. Because distance r is minimum at perihelion, linear orbital speed v is maximum.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q11 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q11: Define acceleration due to gravity (g).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The acceleration acquired by a freely falling body under the sole action of the gravitational attraction of a celestial body (such as Earth) is called <b>acceleration due to gravity</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q12 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q12: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of acceleration due to gravity g.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> metre per second-squared (<b>m/s<sup>2</sup></b> or <b>N/kg</b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q13 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q13: Write the formula connecting g, G, M, and R at Earth&apos;s surface.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q14 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q14: What is the standard numerical value of g at the surface of the Earth at sea level?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The internationally standardized value of g at sea level and 45\\circ latitude is:\n      g = 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup> &approx; 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q15 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q15: How does the value of g vary as we move from the equator towards the poles?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The value of g <b>increases progressively</b> from a minimum of approximately 9.78 m/s<sup>2</sup> at the equator to a maximum of approximately 9.83 m/s<sup>2</sup> at the poles.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q16 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q16: Why is the value of g greater at the poles than at the equator?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> Due to Earth&apos;s equatorial bulge (oblate spheroidal shape), the polar radius is about 21 km shorter than the equatorial radius (R_p &lt; R_e). Because g &prop; 1/R<sup>2</sup>, smaller radius yields larger gravity at the poles (g_p &gt; g_e).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q17 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q17: Write the approximate formula for acceleration due to gravity at height h when h &Lt; R.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g_h &approx; g(1 - (2h / R)) &bull; (Valid strictly when h \\ll R)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q18 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q18: What is the exact formula for acceleration due to gravity at altitude h without binomial approximation?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g_h = (g / (1 + (h / R))<sup>2</sup>) = g((R / R + h))<sup>2</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q19 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q19: Write the formula for acceleration due to gravity at depth d beneath Earth&apos;s surface.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g_d = g(1 - (d / R))\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q20 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q20: What is the value of acceleration due to gravity at the exact center of the Earth?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> <b>Zero</b> (g_{center} = 0). At the center, d = R, so g_d = g(1 - R/R) = 0.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q21 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q21: At what depth d below Earth&apos;s surface does the value of g become half of its surface value?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> At depth <b>d = R/2 (half the Earth&apos;s radius)</b>:\n      g_d = g(1 - (R/2 / R)) = g(1 - (1 / 2)) = (g / 2)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q22 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q22: At what height h is the decrease in g equal to that at a depth d for small distances?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> At height <b>h = d/2</b>. For small distances, &Delta;g_h = g(2h/R) and &Delta;g_d = g(d/R). Setting them equal: 2h = d &rArr; h = d/2.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q23 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q23: Define gravitational field intensity at a spatial point.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>gravitational field intensity</b> <b>E</b>_g at any point is defined as the gravitational force experienced by a unit test mass placed at that point:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <b>E</b>_g = \\lim_{m_0 \\to 0}(<b>F</b> / m_0) = -(G M / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q24 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q24: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of gravitational field intensity.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> Newton per kilogram (<b>N/kg</b>) or <b>m/s<sup>2</sup></b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b> (identical to acceleration)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q25 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q25: Define gravitational potential at a point.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>gravitational potential</b> V at a point in a gravitational field is defined as the work done by an external agent in bringing a unit positive mass from infinity to that point without acceleration:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      V = -(G M / r)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q26 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q26: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of gravitational potential.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      <br/>&bull; <b>SI Unit:</b> Joule per kilogram (<b>J/kg</b> or <b>m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup></b>)\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q27 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q27: Why is gravitational potential always negative?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> By convention, gravitational potential is zero at infinity (V_{\\infty} = 0). Because gravitational attraction is purely attractive, an attractive field does positive work pulling mass inward from infinity, which means the external agent must perform negative work to hold it in check (V &lt; 0).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q28 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q28: Where is gravitational potential taken to be zero by universal convention?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> At <b>infinite distance (r = \\infty)</b> from all mass distributions, where gravitational interaction falls to zero.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q29 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q29: State the relation between gravitational field intensity E_g and gravitational potential V.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> Gravitational field intensity is the negative spatial derivative (potential gradient) of gravitational potential:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      E_g = -(dV / dr)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q30 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q30: Define gravitational potential energy of a body.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>gravitational potential energy</b> U of a body of mass m at distance r from Earth&apos;s center is defined as the work done in bringing the body from infinity to that distance without acceleration.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q31 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q31: Write the formula for the gravitational potential energy of two masses M and m separated by distance r.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      U = -(G M m / r)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q32 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q32: Define escape speed from the surface of a celestial body.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>escape speed</b> is defined as the minimum launch speed required for a projectile projected from the surface of a planet to overcome its gravitational field and never return.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q33 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q33: Write the formulas for escape speed in terms of G, M, and in terms of g, R.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)} = &radic;(2 g R)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q34 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q34: Does the escape speed depend on the mass of the projected projectile?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> <b>No.</b> As shown by v_e = &radic;(2 g R), escape speed depends solely on the mass and radius of the planet, and is completely independent of the mass of the escaping body.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q35 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q35: Does the escape speed depend on the angle of projection?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> <b>No.</b> Escape speed is a scalar quantity derived strictly from mechanical energy conservation, and is independent of the projection angle (provided the trajectory does not intersect the planet).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q36 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q36: What is the numerical value of escape speed from Earth&apos;s surface?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n      v_e = &radic;(2 &times; 9.8 &times; 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 11.2 km/s\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q37 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q37: Why does the Moon lack an atmosphere?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> Because the Moon&apos;s escape speed (v_e &approx; 2.38 km/s) is lower than the root-mean-square thermal speeds of air molecules at lunar temperatures, allowing atmospheric gas molecules to escape into space.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q38 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q38: Define the orbital velocity of an artificial Earth satellite.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> The <b>orbital velocity</b> is the precise horizontal speed required to insert an artificial satellite into a stable circular orbit around the Earth at a given altitude:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_o = &radic;{(G M / R + h)}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q39 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q39: What is the orbital velocity of a satellite revolving very close to Earth&apos;s surface?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> For h \\ll R:\n      v_o = &radic;(g R) = &radic;(9.8 &times; 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 7.92 km/s &approx; 8 km/s\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q40 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q40: State the relationship between escape speed and orbital velocity for a near-Earth satellite.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Answer:</b> \n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_e = &radic;(2) v_o &approx; 1.414 v_o\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      An increase of <b>41.4%</b> in the orbital speed of a near-Earth satellite will cause it to escape Earth&apos;s orbit entirely.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER (SA) QUESTIONS (2 & 3 MARKS EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks Each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Numerical calculations, conceptual deductions, variations of g, gravitational potential problems, and orbital satellite mechanics.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q41 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q41: Two lead spheres of masses 20 kg and 10 kg are placed with their centers 0.5 m apart. Calculate the gravitational force between them.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>Given: m_1 = 20 kg, m_2 = 10 kg, r = 0.5 m, G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup>/kg<sup>2</sup>.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      F = G(m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>) = {(6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; 20 &times; 10}{(0.5)<sup>2</sup>} = {1.335 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup>}{0.25} = 5.34 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> N\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q42 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q42: Calculate the gravitational force of attraction between the Earth (M = 6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup> kg) and the Moon (m = 7.4 &times; 10<sup>22</sup> kg) at distance 3.84 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      F = G(M m / r<sup>2</sup>) = {(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup>) &times; (7.4 &times; 10<sup>22</sup>)}{(3.84 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup>}<br/>\n      = {2.961 &times; 10<sup>37</sup>}{1.475 &times; 10<sup>17</sup>} &approx; 2.01 &times; 10<sup>20</sup> N\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      This enormous force provides the necessary centripetal force keeping the Moon in orbit.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q43 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q43: Three equal masses m are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side a. Find the net gravitational force on any one mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Consider mass m at vertex A. The other two masses at B and C each exert an attractive force of magnitude:\n      F_1 = F_2 = (G m<sup>2</sup> / a<sup>2</sup>)\n      The angle between these two force vectors is 60\\circ. By vector addition:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      F_{net} = &radic;(F_1<sup>2</sup> + F_2<sup>2</sup> + 2 F_1 F_2 \\cos 60\\circ) = &radic;(F<sup>2</sup> + F<sup>2</sup> + 2F<sup>2</sup>(0.5)) = &radic;(3) F = &radic;(3)(G m<sup>2</sup> / a<sup>2</sup>)\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The resultant force acts along the angle bisector toward the centroid of the triangle.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q44 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q44: A planet moves in an elliptical orbit. If the ratio of its maximum distance (aphelion) to minimum distance (perihelion) is 1.5, find the ratio of its maximum to minimum orbital speeds.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Because gravitational force is central, orbital angular momentum is conserved:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      L = m r_1 v_1 = m r_2 v_2 &rArr; {v_{\\max}}{v_{\\min}} = (v_p / v_a) = (r_a / r_p) = {r_{\\max}}{r_{\\min}} = 1.5\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q45 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q45: A planet orbits the Sun with an orbital period of 8 years. Find its orbital radius in terms of Earth&apos;s orbital radius (Astronomical Units).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Applying Kepler&apos;s Third Law: (T_p<sup>2</sup> / T_e<sup>2</sup>) = (r_p<sup>3</sup> / r_e<sup>3</sup>).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      ((8 / 1))<sup>2</sup> = ((r_p / r_e))<sup>3</sup> &rArr; 64 = ((r_p / r_e))<sup>3</sup> &rArr; (r_p / r_e) = (64)<sup>1/3</sup> = 4\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The planet&apos;s orbital radius is <b>4 AU (4 times that of Earth)</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q46 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q46: Calculate the mass of the Sun given Earth&apos;s orbital radius r = 1.5 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> m and period T = 3.15 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> s (1 year).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> From Kepler&apos;s Third Law: T<sup>2</sup> = (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> r<sup>3</sup> / G M_s).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      M_s = (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> r<sup>3</sup> / G T<sup>2</sup>) = {4 &times; (3.14)<sup>2</sup> &times; (1.5 &times; 10<sup>11</sup>)<sup>3</sup>}{(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (3.15 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>)<sup>2</sup>}<br/>\n      = {39.44 &times; 3.375 &times; 10<sup>33</sup>}{6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> &times; 9.92 &times; 10<sup>14</sup>} = {1.331 &times; 10<sup>35</sup>}{6.617 &times; 10<sup>4</sup>} &approx; 2.01 &times; 10<sup>30</sup> kg\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q47 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q47: At what height above the Earth&apos;s surface will the acceleration due to gravity be 25% of its value at the surface?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Here g_h = 0.25 g = g/4. Since the change is large, use the exact formula:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g_h = g((R / R + h))<sup>2</sup> &rArr; (g / 4) = g((R / R + h))<sup>2</sup><br/>\n      (R / R + h) = (1 / 2) &rArr; R + h = 2R &rArr; h = R = 6400 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q48 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q48: Find the height at which the acceleration due to gravity decreases by 1% of its value at the surface (R = 6400 km).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> For a small percentage change (1%), use the binomial approximation:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (&Delta;g / g) = (2h / R) &rArr; 0.01 = (2h / 6400)<br/>\n      2h = 0.01 &times; 6400 = 64 km &rArr; h = 32 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q49 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q49: At what depth below Earth&apos;s surface does the acceleration due to gravity decrease by 20% of its surface value?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> With depth, g_d = g(1 - d/R) &rArr; (&Delta;g / g) = (d / R).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      0.20 = (d / 6400) &rArr; d = 0.20 &times; 6400 = 1280 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q50 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q50: Calculate the percentage decrease in the weight of a body when taken to a height of 32 km above Earth (R = 6400 km).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Weight W = mg &prop; g. Percentage decrease in weight equals percentage decrease in g:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (&Delta;W / W) &times; 100 = (2h / R) &times; 100 = (2 &times; 32 / 6400) &times; 100 = (64 / 64) = 1\\%\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q51 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q51: How far below the surface of the Earth does the acceleration due to gravity become 1% of its surface value?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> We are given g_d = 0.01 g:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      g_d = g(1 - (d / R)) &rArr; 0.01 = 1 - (d / R) &rArr; (d / R) = 0.99<br/>\n      d = 0.99 R = 0.99 &times; 6400 = 6336 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q52 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q52: Compare the acceleration due to gravity at altitude h = R with that at depth d = R/2.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; At altitude h = R: g_h = g((R / R + R))<sup>2</sup> = (g / 4) = 0.25 g.\n      <br/>&bull; At depth d = R/2: g_d = g(1 - (R/2 / R)) = (g / 2) = 0.50 g.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (g_h / g_d) = (g/4 / g/2) = (1 / 2) &rArr; g_d = 2 g_h\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q53 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q53: Calculate the mean density of Earth given g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>, G = 6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> SI units, and R = 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Since g = (4 / 3)&pi; G R &rho;:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &rho; = (3 g / 4&pi; G R) = {3 &times; 9.8}{4 &times; 3.1416 &times; (6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)}<br/>\n      = {29.4}{5.364 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>} &approx; 5480 kg/m<sup>3</sup>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q54 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q54: If the radius of the Earth shrinks by 1% with its mass remaining constant, find the percentage change in g.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> g = G M R<sup>-2</sup>. Differentiating logarithmically:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (&Delta;g / g) = -2(&Delta;R / R) = -2(-1\\%) = +2\\%\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The acceleration due to gravity <b>increases by 2%</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q55 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q55: Two point masses m<sub>1</sub> = 100 kg and m<sub>2</sub> = 900 kg are separated by 1 m. Find the point on the joining line where the net gravitational field is zero.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Let the null point be at distance x from m_1 and (1 - x) from m_2:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (G m_1 / x<sup>2</sup>) = (G m_2 / (1 - x)<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; (100 / x<sup>2</sup>) = (900 / (1 - x)<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; (1 / x) = (3 / 1 - x)<br/>\n      1 - x = 3x &rArr; 4x = 1 &rArr; x = 0.25 m = 25 cm\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The null point lies at <b>25 cm from the 100 kg mass</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q56 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q56: Four particles each of mass 2 kg are placed at the vertices of a square of side 1 m. Calculate the gravitational potential at the center of the square.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Distance from center to each vertex: r = (a / &radic;(2)) = (1 / &radic;(2)) m.\n      <br/>Since potential is a scalar:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      V = 4(-(G m / r)) = -4 (G(2) / 1/&radic;(2)) = -8&radic;(2) G &approx; -8(1.414)(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) = -7.55 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> J/kg\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q57 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q57: Find the work done in bringing a 5 kg mass from infinity to Earth&apos;s surface (M = 6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup> kg, R = 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Work done by external agent without acceleration:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      W_{ext} = U(R) - U(\\infty) = -(G M m / R) - 0<br/>\n      = -{(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup>) &times; 5}{6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>} = -{2.001 &times; 10<sup>15</sup>}{6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>} &approx; -3.13 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> Joules\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      (The gravitational field does +3.13 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> J of positive work).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q58 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q58: Show that the exact change in potential energy reduces to &Delta;U = mgh for small heights h &Lt; R.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      &Delta;U = U(R + h) - U(R) = -(G M m / R + h) - (-(G M m / R)) = G M m((1 / R) - (1 / R + h))\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &Delta;U = (G M m h / R(R + h)) = (G M m h / R<sup>2</sup>(1 + (h / R))) = (m g h / 1 + (h / R))\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      For h \\ll R, the denominator (1 + h/R) &approx; 1, which gives &Delta;U &approx; mgh.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q59 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q59: A body of mass m is raised to height h = R above the surface of the Earth. Find the gain in gravitational potential energy.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Using the exact expression derived in Q58:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &Delta;U = (m g h / 1 + (h / R)) = (m g R / 1 + (R / R)) = (m g R / 2) = (1 / 2) m g R\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q60 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q60: Calculate the escape speed from a planet whose mass is 8 times and radius is 2 times that of the Earth.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Escape speed v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)} &prop; &radic;{(M / R)}.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      {v_{e,p}}{v_{e,E}} = &radic;{(M_p/M_E / R_p/R_E)} = &radic;{(8 / 2)} = &radic;(4) = 2<br/>\n      v_{e,p} = 2 &times; 11.2 km/s = 22.4 km/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q61 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q61: What is the escape speed from the Moon if its mass is 7.36 &times; 10<sup>22</sup> kg and radius is 1.74 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)} = &radic;{{2 &times; (6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (7.36 &times; 10<sup>22</sup>)}{1.74 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>}} = &radic;(5.64 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 2375 m/s &approx; 2.38 km/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q62 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q62: A body is projected vertically upwards with a speed v = 0.5 v<sub>e</sub> from Earth&apos;s surface. What maximum height h does it attain?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> By Conservation of Energy: (1 / 2)m v<sup>2</sup> = &Delta;U = (m g h / 1 + h/R).\n      <br/>Since v_e = &radic;(2 g R), v<sup>2</sup> = (0.5 v_e)<sup>2</sup> = 0.25(2 g R) = 0.5 g R.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (1 / 2)m(0.5 g R) = (m g h / 1 + h/R) &rArr; (R / 4) = (h / 1 + h/R)<br/>\n      R(1 + (h / R)) = 4h &rArr; R + h = 4h &rArr; 3h = R &rArr; h = (R / 3) &approx; 2133 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q63 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q63: A projectile is fired with a speed of 15 km/s from Earth&apos;s surface. What will be its residual speed in deep space far from Earth?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> By Conservation of Mechanical Energy:\n      (1 / 2)m v<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R) = (1 / 2)m v_\\infty<sup>2</sup> + 0\n      Since (G M m / R) = (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_\\infty = &radic;(v<sup>2</sup> - v_e<sup>2</sup>) = &radic;((15)<sup>2</sup> - (11.2)<sup>2</sup>) = &radic;(225 - 125.44) = &radic;(99.56) &approx; 9.98 km/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q64 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q64: An artificial satellite orbits Earth at an altitude of 600 km. Calculate its orbital speed (R = 6400 km, g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Orbital radius r = R + h = 6400 + 600 = 7000 km = 7 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      v_o = &radic;{(g R<sup>2</sup> / R + h)} = &radic;{(9.8 &times; (6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)<sup>2</sup> / 7 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)} = &radic;(5.73 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) &approx; 7570 m/s = 7.57 km/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q65 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q65: Find the orbital period of a satellite orbiting at an altitude of 1600 km above Earth (R = 6400 km).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> r = 6400 + 1600 = 8000 km = 8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      T = 2&pi; &radic;{(r<sup>3</sup> / g R<sup>2</sup>)} = 2(3.1416) &radic;{((8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)<sup>3</sup> / 9.8 &times; (6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)<sup>2</sup>)} = 6.283 &times; 1130 &approx; 7100 s &approx; 1.97 hours\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q66 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q66: Calculate the kinetic energy, potential energy, and total energy of a 200 kg satellite in a circular orbit of radius 8000 km.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> G M m = (6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup>) &times; 200 = 8 &times; 10<sup>16</sup> J m.\n      <br/>Radius r = 8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      K = (G M m / 2 r) = {8 &times; 10<sup>16</sup>}{2(8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)} = 5 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Joules (5 GJ)<br/>\n      U = -(G M m / r) = -{8 &times; 10<sup>16</sup>}{8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>} = -10 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Joules (-10 GJ)<br/>\n      E = K + U = 5 - 10 = -5 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Joules (-5 GJ)\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q67 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q67: What is the binding energy of a satellite of mass 1000 kg orbiting at height h = R?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Radius r = R + h = 2R. Binding Energy:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      B.E. = +(G M m / 2 r) = +(G M m / 4 R) = (m g R / 4) = (1000 &times; 9.8 &times; (6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) / 4) = 1.568 &times; 10<sup>10</sup> J\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q68 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q68: If a satellite in circular orbit at radius r = 2R is suddenly brought to rest, with what speed does it strike Earth&apos;s surface?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Initial energy at rest at r = 2R: E_i = 0 - (G M m / 2R).\n      <br/>Final energy on impact at r = R: E_f = (1 / 2)m v<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R).\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (1 / 2)m v<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R) = -(G M m / 2R) &rArr; (1 / 2)m v<sup>2</sup> = (G M m / 2R) = (1 / 2)m(g R)<br/>\n      v = &radic;(g R) = &radic;(9.8 &times; 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 7.92 km/s\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q69 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q69: Explain why an astronaut feels weightless in an orbiting space station even though gravity is nearly 90% of sea level.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Apparent weight is the normal contact reaction force N exerted by the floor on the astronaut. In orbit, both the space station and the astronaut accelerate toward Earth&apos;s center with the identical free-fall acceleration a = g_h. Thus, m g_h - N = m a = m g_h &rArr; N = 0. The sensation of weightlessness is caused by perpetual common free fall, not the absence of gravity.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q70 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q70: What is a geostationary satellite? State its essential orbital parameters.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> A <b>geostationary satellite</b> revolves around Earth such that it appears permanently stationary over a fixed point on the equator.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Period:</b> Exactly 24 hours (1 sidereal day).\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Sense of Rotation:</b> West to East (matching Earth&apos;s spin).\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Orbital Plane:</b> Coplanar with the Earth&apos;s equatorial plane.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Altitude:</b> Approximately 35,800 km &approx; 36,000 km above the surface.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q71 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q71: Calculate the exact orbital radius and altitude of a geostationary satellite (T = 86400 s).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> From T = 2&pi; &radic;{(r<sup>3</sup> / G M)}:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      r = ((G M T<sup>2</sup> / 4&pi;<sup>2</sup>))<sup>1/3</sup> = ({(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup>) &times; (86400)<sup>2</sup>}{4&pi;<sup>2</sup>})<sup>1/3</sup> &approx; 4.22 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m = 42,200 km<br/>\n      h = r - R = 42,200 - 6400 = 35,800 km\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q72 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q72: Differentiate between Geostationary and Polar satellites.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Geostationary:</b> Orbital plane is equatorial; altitude &approx; 36,000 km; period T = 24 hours; used for telecommunications and television broadcasting.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Polar:</b> Orbital plane passes over North and South poles; low altitude (500 - 800 km); period T &approx; 100 minutes; used for weather forecasting, remote sensing, and environmental surveillance.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q73 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q73: Show that the orbital velocity of a satellite is completely independent of its mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Equating centripetal force to gravitational pull:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (m v_o<sup>2</sup> / r) = (G M m / r<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; v_o<sup>2</sup> = (G M / r) &rArr; v_o = &radic;{(G M / r)}\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Because the satellite mass m cancels from both sides, an apple and a 10-ton satellite in the same orbit travel with identical speeds.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q74 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q74: Show that for a satellite orbiting very close to Earth, its period is T = &radic;(3&pi; / G&rho;).</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> For h \\ll R, r &approx; R. Period T = 2&pi; &radic;{(R<sup>3</sup> / G M)}.\n      <br/>Substitute M = (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      T = 2&pi; &radic;{(R<sup>3</sup> / G ((4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;))} = 2&pi; &radic;{(3 / 4&pi; G &rho;)} = &radic;{(4&pi;<sup>2</sup> &times; 3 / 4&pi; G &rho;)} = &radic;{(3&pi; / G &rho;)}\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q75 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q75: How much additional energy must be supplied to a satellite of mass m in orbit of radius r to make it escape Earth?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> The total mechanical energy in orbit is E_{orbit} = -(G M m / 2 r).\n      <br/>To escape to infinity with zero residual energy, final energy is E_\\infty = 0.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      &Delta;E = E_\\infty - E_{orbit} = 0 - (-(G M m / 2 r)) = +(G M m / 2 r)\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      The energy required is exactly equal to the satellite&apos;s current orbital kinetic energy (K = (G M m / 2r)).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q76 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q76: Distinguish between Inertial Mass and Gravitational Mass.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Inertial Mass (m_i):</b> The measure of a body&apos;s inertia resisting linear acceleration under external force, defined by Newton&apos;s second law: m_i = F/a. Measured using an inertial balance.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Gravitational Mass (m_g):</b> The measure of a body&apos;s gravitational interaction with other masses, defined by Newton&apos;s law of gravitation: m_g = F r<sup>2</sup> / (G M). Measured using a physical beam balance.\n      <br/>Einstein&apos;s Principle of Equivalence establishes that m_i = m_g to within experimental precision.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q77 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q77: Why does a person weigh slightly more at the poles than at the equator? State two reasons.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Oblate Spheroid Shape:</b> Earth&apos;s polar radius R_p is about 21 km smaller than equatorial radius R_e. Since g &prop; 1/R<sup>2</sup>, gravity is naturally higher at the poles.\n      <br/>2. <b>Axial Rotation of Earth:</b> Centrifugal force due to Earth&apos;s spin reduces effective gravity at latitude &lambda;: g&apos; = g - R\\omega<sup>2</sup>\\cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda;. At the equator (&lambda; = 0\\circ), reduction is maximum (R\\omega<sup>2</sup>), whereas at the poles (&lambda; = 90\\circ), centrifugal reduction is zero.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q78 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q78: If Earth stops rotating on its axis, what will be the effect on g at the equator and at the poles?</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> Effective gravity is g&apos; = g - R\\omega<sup>2</sup>\\cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda;.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>At Equator (lambda = 0<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> When rotation halts (omega = 0), centrifugal reduction ceases, so g <b>increases by R\\omega<sup>2</sup> approx 0.034\text{ m/s}<sup>2</sup></b>.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>At Poles (lambda = 90<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> Since cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0, centrifugal force was already zero, so g <b>remains completely unchanged</b>.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q79 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q79: Two satellites of masses m<sub>1</sub> and m<sub>2</sub> revolve in orbits of radii r<sub>1</sub> and r<sub>2</sub> with r<sub>1</sub> = 4 r<sub>2</sub>. Find the ratio of their orbital speeds and periods.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Orbital speed v_o &prop; (1 / &radic;(r)):\n      (v_1 / v_2) = &radic;{(r_2 / r_1)} = &radic;{(1 / 4)} = (1 / 2)\n      &bull; Period T &prop; r<sup>3/2</sup>:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (T_1 / T_2) = ((r_1 / r_2))<sup>3/2</sup> = (4)<sup>3/2</sup> = 8\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q80 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 15px;\">Q80: Derive the formula for the escape speed of a body projected from the center of the Earth.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Solution:</b> The potential at Earth&apos;s center is V_{center} = -(3 G M / 2 R).\n      <br/>Initial energy of body launched from center with speed v_{e,c}:\n      E_i = (1 / 2)m v_{e,c}<sup>2</sup> + m V_{center} = (1 / 2)m v_{e,c}<sup>2</sup> - (3 G M m / 2 R)\n      To reach infinity with zero kinetic energy, E_f = 0:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      (1 / 2)m v_{e,c}<sup>2</sup> = (3 G M m / 2 R) &rArr; v_{e,c} = &radic;{(3 G M / R)} = &radic;(3 g R) = &radic;{(3 / 2)} v_e &approx; 1.225 v_e\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Escape speed from Earth&apos;s center is &radic;(3 g R) &approx; 13.7 km/s.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER (LA) QUESTIONS & DERIVATIONS (5 MARKS EACH) -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FDC830; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 25px 0 15px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      SECTION C: Long Answer (LA) Questions & Derivations (5 Marks Each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\n      Exhaustive mathematical derivations, calculus integrations, altitude & depth variations, escape speed proofs, and orbital satellite mechanics.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q81 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q81: State Kepler&apos;s laws of planetary motion. Rigorously derive Kepler&apos;s second law (Law of Areas) from the principle of conservation of angular momentum.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Kepler&apos;s Laws:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Law of Orbits:</b> All planets revolve around the Sun in elliptical orbits, with the Sun situated at one of the foci.\n      <br/>2. <b>Law of Areas:</b> The radius vector drawn from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time (dA/dt = constant).\n      <br/>3. <b>Law of Periods:</b> T<sup>2</sup> &prop; a<sup>3</sup>, where T is the orbital period and a is the semi-major axis.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Central Force Nature of Gravitation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The gravitational pull exerted by the Sun (mass M_s) on a planet (mass m) acts along the radial line joining them: <b>F</b> = -(G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r} The torque acting on the planet about the Sun: <b>&tau;</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>F</b> = <b>r</b> &times; (-(G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}) = <b>0</b> since the cross product of any vector with its own collinear unit vector vanishes.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Angular Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By Newton&apos;s second law in rotational form: <b>&tau;</b> = (d<b>L</b> / dt) = <b>0</b> &rArr; <b>L</b> = <b>r</b> &times; <b>p</b> = m(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>) = constant vector</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Area Swept Out in Time dt</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In differential time dt, the displacement of the planet is d<b>r</b> = <b>v</b>\\,dt. The triangular area swept out by the radius vector is: d<b>A</b> = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; d<b>r</b>) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>\\,dt)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Areal Velocity Expression</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Dividing both sides by dt: (d<b>A</b> / dt) = (1 / 2)(<b>r</b> &times; <b>v</b>) = (<b>r</b> &times; m<b>v</b> / 2m) = (<b>L</b> / 2m) Since both <b>L</b> and m are constants of the motion: (dA / dt) = (L / 2m) = constant This completely proves Kepler&apos;s second law.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q82 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q82: Deduce Kepler&apos;s third law of planetary motion for circular planetary orbits using Newton&apos;s universal law of gravitation.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider a planet of mass m revolving in a circular orbit of radius r around the Sun of mass M_s with constant orbital speed v.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Centripetal Force Balance</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The necessary centripetal force required to keep the planet in circular motion is provided solely by the gravitational pull of the Sun: (m v<sup>2</sup> / r) = (G M_s m / r<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; v<sup>2</sup> = (G M_s / r) &rArr; v = &radic;{(G M_s / r)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Orbital Period Relation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The time period T required for one complete revolution around the orbit of circumference 2&pi; r is: T = (2&pi; r / v) = {2&pi; r}{&radic;{(G M_s / r)}} = {2&pi; r<sup>3/2</sup>}{&radic;(G M_s)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Squaring Both Sides</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">T<sup>2</sup> = (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> r<sup>3</sup> / G M_s) = ((4&pi;<sup>2</sup> / G M_s)) r<sup>3</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conclusion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since G and M_s are constant, (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> / G M_s) = constant, establishing that: T<sup>2</sup> &prop; r<sup>3</sup> (or T<sup>2</sup> &prop; a<sup>3</sup> for elliptical orbits).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q83 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q83: State Newton&apos;s Universal Law of Gravitation. Express it in vector form and discuss its four fundamental characteristics.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Universal Law Statement:</b> Every particle in the universe attracts every other particle with a force directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them:\n      F = G(m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\n      In vector form, the force <b>F</b>_{12} exerted on particle 2 by particle 1 is:\n      <b>F</b>_{12} = -(G m_1 m_2 / r<sup>2</sup>)\\hat{r}_{12} = -(G m_1 m_2 / r<sup>3</sup>)<b>r</b>_{12}\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-left: 3px solid #FDC830; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 10px 0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Four Fundamental Characteristics of Gravitational Force:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Universal Attraction:</b> Gravitational force is always attractive; unlike electrostatic forces, repulsive gravitation does not exist in classical physics.\n      <br/>2. <b>Inverse Square Law:</b> The force obeys F &prop; 1/r<sup>2</sup>. Doubling the separation quarters the mutual force.\n      <br/>3. <b>Central and Conservative Nature:</b> It acts strictly along the line joining the centers of the two interacting masses, and the work done around any closed path is zero (\\oint <b>F</b>\\cdot d<b>r</b> = 0).\n      <br/>4. <b>Medium Independence & Action-Reaction Pair:</b> The force is completely unaltered by the intervening physical medium (air, water, vacuum) and obeys Newton&apos;s third law: <b>F</b>_{21} = -<b>F</b>_{12}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q84 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q84: Describe the principle, working, and mathematical formulation of the Cavendish Torsion Balance experiment to determine G.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Principle:</b> The gravitational attraction between two pairs of spherical masses generates a deflecting couple on a light horizontal rod suspended by a fine quartz or phosphor-bronze wire. The deflecting gravitational torque is balanced by the restoring elastic twist torque of the suspension wire.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Experimental Arrangement</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Two small lead spheres each of mass m are attached to the opposite ends of a light rod of length L. Two large lead spheres each of mass M are brought close to the small spheres on opposite sides at separation d.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Gravitational Deflecting Torque</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Each small sphere experiences an attractive force F = (G M m / d<sup>2</sup>). Since these forces act on opposite ends of the rod, they form a couple of arm L: \\tau_{def} = F &times; L = (G M m L / d<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Restoring Torque of Suspension Fiber</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">If the wire twists through angle &theta;, the restoring torque is: \\tau_{res} = C &theta; where C is the restoring torque per unit twist (torsional rigidity).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Equilibrium Condition & Formula for G</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At equilibrium, \\tau_{def} = \\tau_{res}: (G M m L / d<sup>2</sup>) = C &theta; &rArr; G = (C &theta; d<sup>2</sup> / M m L) Measuring the twist &theta; via a lamp-and-scale mirror arrangement yields G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup>/kg<sup>2</sup>.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q85 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q85: Derive the relation between g and G at Earth&apos;s surface. Deduce the formula for the mean density of the Earth.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider the Earth to be a homogeneous sphere of mass M, radius R, and uniform volume mass density \rho.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Equating Weight to Gravitational Attraction</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">A test body of mass m placed on Earth&apos;s surface experiences gravitational pull: F = (G M m / R<sup>2</sup>) By Newton&apos;s second law, this gravitational force is the body&apos;s true weight: F = mg. Equating both: m g = (G M m / R<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Expressing Mass in Terms of Mean Density</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The volume of the Earth is V = (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup>. Mass of Earth: M = V &rho; = (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Substituting Mass into Gravity Formula</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">g = (G / R<sup>2</sup>)((4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup> &rho;) = (4 / 3)&pi; G R &rho;</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Mean Density Formula & Numerical Evaluation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">&rho; = (3 g / 4&pi; G R) Substituting g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>, G = 6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> SI, R = 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m: &rho; = {3 &times; 9.8}{4 &times; 3.1416 &times; (6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)} &approx; 5500 kg/m<sup>3</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q86 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q86: Derive an expression for the acceleration due to gravity at altitude h. Obtain the binomial approximation for h &Lt; R and find fractional decrease.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>At Earth&apos;s surface (r = R): g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>).\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Exact Expression at Altitude h</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At height h above the surface, distance from center is r = R + h: g_h = (G M / (R + h)<sup>2</sup>) = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>(1 + (h / R))<sup>2</sup>) = (g / (1 + (h / R))<sup>2</sup>) This is the exact formula valid for any height h.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Binomial Expansion for h << R</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Rewrite as a negative exponent: g_h = g(1 + (h / R))<sup>-2</sup> Applying the Binomial Theorem (1 + x)<sup>n</sup> = 1 + n x + \\dots for |x| \\ll 1: g_h &approx; g(1 - (2h / R))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Fractional and Percentage Variations</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Absolute decrease in g: &Delta;g = g - g_h = g((2h / R)) Fractional decrease: (&Delta;g / g) = (2h / R) Percentage decrease: ((&Delta;g / g)) &times; 100 = ((2h / R)) &times; 100\\%</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q87 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q87: Derive an expression for acceleration due to gravity at depth d below Earth&apos;s surface. Prove that g vanishes at Earth&apos;s center.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Let the Earth have radius R, mass M, and uniform density \rho = (M / (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup>).\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Spherical Shell Theorem Application</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At depth d below the surface, the distance from Earth&apos;s center is r = R - d. The outer concentric spherical shell of thickness d exerts zero resultant gravitational force on any interior mass. Gravitational attraction is generated exclusively by the inner core sphere of radius r = R - d.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Enclosed Core Mass M&apos;</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">M&apos; = (4 / 3)&pi; (R - d)<sup>3</sup> &rho; = (4 / 3)&pi; (R - d)<sup>3</sup> ((M / (4 / 3)&pi; R<sup>3</sup>)) = M ((R - d)<sup>3</sup> / R<sup>3</sup>)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Evaluating Acceleration at Depth d</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">g_d = (G M&apos; / (R - d)<sup>2</sup>) = (G / (R - d)<sup>2</sup>)[M ((R - d)<sup>3</sup> / R<sup>3</sup>)] = (G M / R<sup>3</sup>)(R - d) Since g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>): g_d = (g / R)(R - d) = g(1 - (d / R))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Gravity at Center of Earth</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At the center of the Earth, d = R: g_{center} = g(1 - (R / R)) = g(0) = 0 The acceleration due to gravity is identically zero at Earth&apos;s center.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q88 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q88: Compare the variations of g with altitude and depth. Prove that for small distances, a given decrease in g at height h equals the decrease at depth d = 2h.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Proof and Comparative Analysis:</b>\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Formulas for Altitude and Depth</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For small height h \\ll R: g_h = g(1 - (2h / R)) &rArr; &Delta;g_h = g - g_h = g((2h / R)) For depth d: g_d = g(1 - (d / R)) &rArr; &Delta;g_d = g - g_d = g((d / R))</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Equating the Decreases</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Setting the two decreases equal: &Delta;g_h = &Delta;g_d: g((2h / R)) = g((d / R)) &rArr; 2h = d &rArr; d = 2h \\quad or \\quad h = (d / 2)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Physical Interpretation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Acceleration due to gravity drops twice as rapidly with height as it does with depth. For example, the decrease in g observed at a height of 10 km above the surface is equal to the decrease found at a depth of 20 km inside a mine.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q89 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q89: Derive the expression for the variation of acceleration due to gravity with latitude &lambda; due to the rotation of the Earth: g&apos; = g - R&omega;<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda;.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Consider a particle of mass m on Earth&apos;s surface at latitude &lambda;. The Earth rotates about its polar axis with constant angular velocity \\omega.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Radius of Circular Path of Rotation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">As Earth spins, the particle describes a horizontal circle of radius: r = R \\cos&lambda;</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Centrifugal Force in Rotating Frame</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In Earth&apos;s rotating frame of reference, the particle experiences an outward fictitious centrifugal force: F_c = m r \\omega<sup>2</sup> = m (R \\cos&lambda;) \\omega<sup>2</sup> = m R \\omega<sup>2</sup> \\cos&lambda; directed horizontally away from the rotation axis.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Vector Resolution along the Radial Line</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The true gravitational force m g acts radially toward Earth&apos;s center. The component of centrifugal force acting radially outward against gravity is: F_{c,r} = F_c \\cos&lambda; = (m R \\omega<sup>2</sup> \\cos&lambda;) \\cos&lambda; = m R \\omega<sup>2</sup> \\cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda;</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Effective Gravity Formula</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Apparent weight: m g&apos; = m g - m R \\omega<sup>2</sup> \\cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda; &rArr; g&apos; = g - R \\omega<sup>2</sup> \\cos<sup>2</sup>&lambda;.<br/>&bull; <b>At Equator (lambda = 0<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = 1 &rArr; g_e = g - R\\omega<sup>2</sup> (minimum).<br/>&bull; <b>At Poles (lambda = 90<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0 &rArr; g_p = g (maximum).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q90 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q90: Define Gravitational Potential Energy. Rigorously derive U(r) = -GMm/r by calculus integration and explain the physical meaning of the negative sign.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Definition:</b> The gravitational potential energy of a body at a point is the work done by an external agent in bringing the body from infinity to that point without acceleration.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Differential Work Element</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let a test mass m be at distance x from the center of Earth (mass M). The attractive gravitational force on m is: F = (G M m / x<sup>2</sup>) directed radially inward toward Earth&apos;s center.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Infinitesimal Displacement</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Work done by an external agent against the attractive force in displacing the body inward through dx toward Earth without acceleration: dW = -F\\,dx = -(G M m / x<sup>2</sup>)\\,dx</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Definite Integral from Infinity to r</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">U(r) = \\int_\\infty<sup>r</sup> -(G M m / x<sup>2</sup>)\\,dx = -G M m \\int_\\infty<sup>r</sup> x<sup>-2</sup>\\,dx = -G M m [ -(1 / x) ]_\\infty<sup>r</sup> = G M m [ (1 / r) - (1 / \\infty) ] U(r) = -(G M m / r)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Physical Significance of the Negative Sign</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The negative sign indicates that the system is <b>bound</b>. Gravitational force is attractive, so positive work was done by the field itself in bringing the mass from infinity. An external supply of positive energy +GMm/r (Binding Energy) must be provided to free the body to infinite separation where U = 0.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q91 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q91: Define Gravitational Potential. Derive V = -GM/r and establish the gradient relation E<sub>g</sub> = -dV/dr.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Definition:</b> Gravitational potential V at a point is the work done in bringing a unit positive test mass from infinity to that point without acceleration: V = U/m.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Derivation of V = -GM/r</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Setting m = 1 kg in the potential energy derivation: V(r) = (U(r) / m) = (-G M m / r / m) = -(G M / r)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Relation between Gravitational Field and Potential</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Work done in moving a unit test mass through displacement dr against field <b>E</b>_g: dV = -<b>E</b>_g \\cdot d<b>r</b> = -E_g\\,dr &rArr; E_g = -(dV / dr)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Verification</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">E_g = -(d / dr)(-(G M / r)) = -((G M / r<sup>2</sup>)) = -(G M / r<sup>2</sup>) matching the magnitude and inward direction of the gravitational field.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q92 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q92: Define Escape Speed. Rigorously derive v<sub>e</sub> = &radic;(2GM/R) = &radic;(2gR) using energy conservation.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Definition:</b> The minimum initial projection speed required for a body to overcome Earth&apos;s gravitational field and never return.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Total Mechanical Energy at Surface</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let a body of mass m be projected with speed v_e from Earth&apos;s surface (r = R): E_i = K_i + U_i = (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Total Mechanical Energy at Infinity</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At infinity (r \\to \\infty), gravitational potential energy vanishes (U_f = 0). For minimum launch speed, the body just reaches infinity with zero remaining velocity (K_f = 0): E_f = K_f + U_f = 0 + 0 = 0</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Applying Law of Conservation of Energy</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In a conservative field, E_i = E_f: (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> - (G M m / R) = 0 &rArr; (1 / 2)m v_e<sup>2</sup> = (G M m / R) &rArr; v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Expressing in Terms of Surface Gravity g</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since g = (G M / R<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; G M = g R<sup>2</sup>: v_e = &radic;{(2(g R<sup>2</sup>) / R)} = &radic;(2 g R) Substituting Earth values: v_e = &radic;(2 &times; 9.8 &times; 6.4 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &approx; 11.2 km/s.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q93 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q93: Explain in detail why the Moon lacks an atmosphere while Earth retains a dense gaseous envelope, using kinetic molecular theory.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Theoretical Explanation:</b>\n      <br/>Retention of an atmosphere on any celestial body depends on the competition between the <b>escape speed (v_e)</b> of the planet and the <b>root-mean-square thermal speed (v_{rms})</b> of gas molecules at surface temperatures.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Thermal Molecular Speed Formula</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">From the kinetic theory of gases, the root-mean-square speed of gas molecules of molar mass M_{mol} at absolute temperature T is: v_{rms} = &radic;{{3 R T}{M_{mol}}} For Earth&apos;s atmosphere (T &approx; 300 K), v_{rms} for Nitrogen (N_2) is &approx; 0.52 km/s and for Oxygen (O_2) is &approx; 0.48 km/s.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Atmospheric Retention Condition on Earth</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">On Earth, escape speed is v_e &approx; 11.2 km/s. Since v_{rms} \\ll v_e (thermal speed is less than 5% of escape speed), gas molecules cannot escape into space, retaining a dense atmosphere.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Atmospheric Escape on the Moon</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">On the Moon, mass and surface gravity are low, giving an escape speed of only v_{e,Moon} &approx; 2.38 km/s. During lunar daytime, temperatures reach up to 400 K, elevating molecular thermal speeds above v_{e,Moon}. Consequently, gas molecules easily exceeded escape speed and boiled away into interplanetary space over billions of years.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q94 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q94: Derive expressions for the orbital velocity, time period, and orbital frequency of an artificial satellite orbiting Earth at altitude h.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Derivation:</b>\n      <br/>Let a satellite of mass m orbit Earth (mass M, radius R) in a circular orbit at altitude h (r = R + h).\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">1. Orbital Velocity (v_o)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Centripetal force is provided by gravitational attraction: (m v_o<sup>2</sup> / R + h) = (G M m / (R + h)<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; v_o = &radic;{(G M / R + h)} = &radic;{(g R<sup>2</sup> / R + h)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">2. Orbital Period (T)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">T = (2&pi; (R + h) / v_o) = {2&pi; (R + h)}{&radic;{(G M / R + h)}} = {2&pi; (R + h)<sup>3/2</sup>}{&radic;(G M)} = 2&pi; &radic;{((R + h)<sup>3</sup> / g R<sup>2</sup>)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">3. Orbital Frequency (f)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">f = (1 / T) = (1 / 2&pi;)&radic;{(G M / (R + h)<sup>3</sup>)} = (R / 2&pi;)&radic;{(g / (R + h)<sup>3</sup>)}</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q95 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q95: Establish the relation between escape speed v<sub>e</sub> and near-Earth orbital velocity v<sub>o</sub>: v<sub>e</sub> = &radic;2 v<sub>o</sub>. Explain its physical significance.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Mathematical Proof:</b>\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Near-Earth Orbital Velocity</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For a satellite orbiting very close to Earth (h \\ll R): v_o = &radic;{(G M / R)} = &radic;(g R)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Escape Speed from Earth's Surface</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">v_e = &radic;{(2 G M / R)} = &radic;(2 g R)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Ratio and Relation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">(v_e / v_o) = (&radic;(2 g R) / &radic;(g R)) = &radic;(2) &rArr; v_e = &radic;(2) v_o &approx; 1.414 v_o</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Physical Significance</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">This demonstrates that an increase in the speed of an orbiting satellite by a factor of &radic;(2) - 1 &approx; 41.4\\% will supply enough kinetic energy to break its closed elliptical/circular orbit and launch it onto an open parabolic escape trajectory out of Earth&apos;s gravitational grip.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q96 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q96: Derive expressions for the Kinetic Energy, Potential Energy, Total Energy, and Binding Energy of an orbiting satellite. State their numerical ratios.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b>Energy Derivations:</b>\n      <br/>For a satellite of mass m orbiting at radius r with speed v_o = &radic;(G M / r):\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">1. Kinetic Energy (K)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">K = (1 / 2)m v_o<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)m ((G M / r)) = (G M m / 2 r)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">2. Potential Energy (U)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">U = -(G M m / r)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">3. Total Mechanical Energy (E)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">E = K + U = (G M m / 2 r) - (G M m / r) = -(G M m / 2 r)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">4. Binding Energy (B.E.) & Energy Ratios</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">B.E. = -E = +(G M m / 2 r) Ratios: K : U : E = 1 : -2 : -1 \\quad &rArr; \\quad E = -K = (U / 2)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q97 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q97: What is a Geostationary Satellite? Derive the formula for its height above the Earth&apos;s surface.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Definition:</b> A satellite whose orbital period matches the Earth&apos;s rotational period (T = 24 hours), orbiting in the equatorial plane from West to East so that it remains permanently stationary relative to a ground observer.\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Formula for Orbital Radius r</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">From Kepler&apos;s Third Law: T<sup>2</sup> = (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> r<sup>3</sup> / G M) &rArr; r<sup>3</sup> = (G M T<sup>2</sup> / 4&pi;<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; r = ((G M T<sup>2</sup> / 4&pi;<sup>2</sup>))<sup>1/3</sup></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Numerical Substitution</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Given T = 24 h = 86400 s, M = 6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup> kg, G = 6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> SI: r = ({(6.67 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>) &times; (6 &times; 10<sup>24</sup>) &times; (86400)<sup>2</sup>}{4 &times; (3.1416)<sup>2</sup>})<sup>1/3</sup> &approx; 4.22 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m = 42,200 km</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Altitude above Earth's Surface (h)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">h = r - R = 42,200 km - 6,400 km = 35,800 km &approx; 36,000 km</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q98 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q98: Construct a comprehensive comparison between Geostationary and Polar Satellites across key astronomical and engineering parameters.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Tabular Comparison:</b>\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"overflow-x: auto;\">\n      <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 13px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n        <thead>\n          <tr style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830;\">\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Parameter</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Geostationary Satellite (GEO)</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); text-align: left;\">Polar Satellite (LEO)</th>\n          </tr>\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Orbital Plane</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Equatorial plane (inclination 0<sup>c</sup>irc)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Passes over North & South poles (inclination approx 90<sup>c</sup>irc)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Altitude Above Surface</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">approx 35,800\text{ km} (high altitude)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">500 - 800\text{ km} (low altitude)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Orbital Period</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Exactly 24 hours</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">approx 100\text{ minutes} (14-15 orbits per day)</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Relative Motion</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Appears stationary over a fixed ground point</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Scans successive strips of Earth as Earth rotates underneath</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Earth Coverage</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Covers approx 42% of Earth (poor polar view)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Covers the entire globe including Arctic & Antarctic</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Primary Uses</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Telecommunication, television relay, continuous weather monitoring</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">Remote sensing, spy/military reconnaissance, climate change, cartography</td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q99 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q99: Explain the true physical cause of Weightlessness in an orbiting satellite. Formulate the equation of motion for an astronaut.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Theory of Weightlessness:</b>\n      <br/>It is a common student misconception that weightlessness in orbit is caused by zero gravity. In reality, at an altitude of 400\text{ km} (e.g. International Space Station), gravity is g_h = g(R/(R+h))<sup>2</sup> approx 8.7\text{ m/s}<sup>2</sup>—nearly <b>90%</b> of sea level gravity!\n    </p>\n    \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation & Proof</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Equation of Motion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Consider an astronaut of mass m standing on a weighing scale on the floor of a spacecraft orbiting at altitude h. Both the spacecraft and the astronaut have acceleration a = g_h directed toward Earth&apos;s center. The downward forces on the astronaut are gravity m g_h and the upward normal contact reaction N from the floor: m g_h - N = m a</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Free-Fall Condition</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Because the spacecraft is in free fall under gravity, a = g_h: m g_h - N = m g_h &rArr; N = 0</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.2); color: #FDC830; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FDC830;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FDC830; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conclusion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since human sensation of weight originates from the normal reaction force N exerted by a supporting floor or seat, when N = 0, the body experiences complete physiological and mechanical <b>weightlessness</b>. The astronaut and satellite are falling together with identical acceleration.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q100 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(253, 200, 48, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FDC830; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 16px;\">Q100: Master Synthesis of Unit VI: Gravitation. Consolidate all mathematical laws, potential relationships, and examination pitfalls.</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #E2E8F0;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">Master Summary of Gravitational Laws:</b>\n      <br/>1. <b>Newton&apos;s Gravitation:</b> F = G\frac{m_1 m_2}{r<sup>2</sup>} &bull; G = 6.674 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup>\text{ N m}<sup>2</sup>\text{/kg}<sup>2</sup> [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].\n      <br/>2. <b>Surface Gravity:</b> g = \frac{GM}{R<sup>2</sup>} = \frac{4}{3}pi G R \rho &bull; \rho = \frac{3g}{4pi GR}.\n      <br/>3. <b>Variations of g:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Altitude (h): Exact g_h = \frac{g}{(1 + h/R)<sup>2</sup>} &bull; Binomial (h ll R): g_h = gleft(1 - \frac{2h}{R}\right).\n      <br/>&bull; Depth (d): g_d = gleft(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right) &bull; Center: g = 0.\n      <br/>&bull; Equator to Poles: g_p > g_e due to oblate shape and axial spin (g' = g - Romega<sup>2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>lambda).\n      <br/>4. <b>Potential & Energy:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Gravitational Potential: V = -\frac{GM}{r} (J/kg) &bull; Field relation: E_g = -\frac{dV}{dr}.\n      <br/>&bull; Gravitational Potential Energy: U = -\frac{GMm}{r} (J) &bull; Surface elevation Delta U = \frac{mgh}{1 + h/R}.\n      <br/>5. <b>Escape & Orbital Dynamics:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; Escape Speed: v_e = sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}} = sqrt{2gR} approx 11.2\text{ km/s}.\n      <br/>&bull; Orbital Speed: v_o = sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} = sqrt{\frac{gR<sup>2</sup>}{R+h}} &bull; Near-Earth v_o = sqrt{gR} approx 7.92\text{ km/s}.\n      <br/>&bull; Inter-relation: v_e = sqrt{2} v_o.\n      <br/>&bull; Satellite Energies: K = \frac{GMm}{2r}, U = -\frac{GMm}{r}, E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}, \text{B.E.} = +\frac{GMm}{2r}.\n      <br/><br/>\n      <b style=\"color: #FDC830;\">High-Yield Board Traps:</b>\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Altitude vs Depth rate:</b> Gravity drops twice as fast with height as it does with depth (Delta g_h / g = 2h/R vs Delta g_d / g = d/R).\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Escape speed independence:</b> v_e does NOT depend on the mass or launch angle of the projected object.\n      <br/>&bull; <b>Satellite Total Energy:</b> Total energy is negative (E = -K$), indicating a bound gravitational system.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n\n</div>";

